{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"tid-1","run_id":"run-1","tool_use_id":"toolu-1","status":"completed","output_file":"/tmp/claude-tmp/-tmp-work-r8-after-budget-interrupt-1/sess-1/tasks/tid-1.output","summary":"The Keeper of Gray Rock\n\nFor thirty-one years, Elias Vane had climbed the same one hundred and twelve iron steps every evening, and for thirty-one years he had never once counted them aloud. Counting was for people who expected the stairs to end. He knew they would not end. They would simply stop being his.\n\nThe lighthouse stood on Gray Rock, a knuckle of basalt two miles off the coast of Harrow's Reach, where the sea never fully agreed with the land. The mainland sent a supply boat every third Tuesday. The boat brought flour, lamp oil, paraffin, letters when there were letters, and a young man named Pim who talked too much and left too quickly.\n\nThis autumn, the letters had stopped.\n\nElias told himself it was the storms. Three gales in September had torn the cable line from the signal post, and the telegraph hummed nothing but silence. He repaired what he could. He wound the great lens each night, polished the brass each morning, and wrote his logbook in the careful slant his mother had taught him: *Wind NW, force six. Visibility good. Light lit at dusk. Nothing to report.*\n\nBut on the eleventh of October, something was reported.\n\nA small boat appeared at dawn, drifting without oars, its painted name scraped away. Elias rowed out to it through a chop that stung his eyes. Inside lay a girl of perhaps nine, wrapped in a sailcloth coat, clutching a tin box against her chest. She did not cry when he lifted her. She only looked past him, toward the tower, as if she had been told where to look.\n\n\"My father said the light would be on,\" she whispered.\n\nHe carried her up the iron steps. He counted them this time, under his breath, and lost count somewhere near the seventy-third.\n\nHer name was Ada. The tin box held a child's treasures: a blue marble, a pressed flower, and a folded map of the coast with a single circle drawn in red around Gray Rock. Her father, she explained, was a lamplighter from the southern towns who had taken work on a ship bound north. The ship had gone down in the September gale. He had told her, before he left, that if the world ever went wrong, she should find the light that never went out.\n\nElias did not know what to say to that. He made her cocoa on the small iron stove and let her sleep in the cot below the lamp room, where the great beam swept over the water every eleven seconds, steady as a heartbeat.\n\nIn the weeks that followed, Ada learned the rhythms of the tower. She learned to trim the wicks, to read the barometer, to hum while she polished. She asked questions he had not been asked in decades. Why does the light turn? Who decided the number of steps? Does the sea ever get tired?\n\n\"No,\" Elias said. \"But it changes its mind sometimes.\"\n\nBy November, the supply boat had still not come. The letters had not resumed. Elias began to understand that the mainland might be quiet for reasons he could not fix, and that the light had never been only for ships. It was for whoever was out there, lost in the dark, looking for a steady point to steer by.\n\nOn the first clear night of the month, Ada climbed the stairs beside him, counting under her breath, and this time Elias joined in. They reached the lamp room together at one hundred and twelve.\n\nHe opened the great shutter. The beam rose into the cold and swept out across the water, reaching for anything that might still be looking back.\n\n\"There,\" Ada said, pointing. Far to the east, a single light answered theirs, small and faint, blinking in a pattern that was not quite a signal but was not quite nothing either.\n\nElias wrote in the logbook that night, in the careful slant of his mother's hand: *Wind light, sea calm. Light lit at dusk. Another light seen to the east. Company.*\n\nFor the first time in thirty-one years, he did not count the steps going down.","usage":{"total_tokens":6412,"tool_uses":0,"duration_ms":8876},"uuid":"uuid-56","session_id":"sess-1"}
